+A small ASP.NET Core (net8.0) minimal API for managing work items: create, list, update, archive
+and delete tasks, each with a state (`Open`, `InProgress`, `Done`), a priority (`Low`, `Medium`,
 `High`), an optional due date and timestamps. Storage is an in-memory store seeded with a few
 sample tasks; all `/tasks` routes require a bearer token.
 
@@ -38,6 +38,47 @@ and returned on every task. It is optional on both POST and PUT: omit it, or sen
 task has no due date. Because PUT replaces the whole task, a PUT body without `dueDate` clears any
 due date the task had - send the current value back to keep it.
 
+## Archiving
+
+A task that is finished with, but worth keeping, can be filed away instead of deleted:
+
+```bash
+curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" http://localhost:5080/tasks/$ID/archive
+curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" http://localhost:5080/tasks/$ID/unarchive
+```
+
+Both return the task; both are idempotent, so a retry is never an error. Archiving a task that is
+already archived does **not** move its `archivedAt` - the field records when the task was filed
+away, not when the button was last pressed.
+
+Every task carries a nullable `archivedAt`: `null` while it is on the working list, an ISO-8601
+instant once it is archived. Archiving is independent of `status` - `Done` says the work finished,
+archiving says the task should stop cluttering the list, and a task can be either without being
+the other. Nothing is destroyed: an archived task keeps its content and stays readable at
+`GET /tasks/{id}`.
+
+`GET /tasks` takes `?archived=`, which decides which side of the archive a listing shows:
+
+| Value | Returns |
+| --- | --- |
+| omitted, or `false` | the working list - archived tasks left out |
+| `true` | only archived tasks |
+| `all` | both |
+
+The value is case-insensitive, and those three words are the whole vocabulary: anything else
+(`yes`, `1`, an empty value) is a **400**, the same strictness `?priority=` already applies. The
+two filters combine - `?archived=true&priority=High` is the high-priority part of the archive - and
+neither widens the ownership scope: the archive is still only ever the caller's own.
+
+`DELETE /tasks/{id}` is unchanged and still destroys the task, archived or not. Archiving is the
+reversible way to clear the list; delete is for when the task really should be gone.
+
+One seeded sample task ("Write the project README") ships archived, so a fresh checkout shows both
+sides of the feature rather than an empty archive that looks broken.
+
+> **Behaviour change.** `GET /tasks` no longer returns every task the caller owns: archived ones
+> are left out unless asked for. A client that wants the previous listing sends `?archived=all`.
+
